VERDICT: PASS -- in all 9 speed, duplex and auto-negotiation combinations, the 4050 decoded
         the DUT's MAC/PHY TLV with the expected values.
  Method (the database has no steps): both ends run Link Layer Discovery Protocol (LLDP).
  Turn on its IEEE 802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status TLV (Type-Length-Value) at both ends.
  Set 9 combinations, C1-C9, on the DUT port. For each one, read `show lldp neighbors detail` on
  the 4050. The forced rows C4-C7 set the 4050 the same way, so the link is up. At C4-C7
  the DUT also decodes the 4050's TLV (the reverse direction).
  Expected with auto-negotiation on: "Enabled", the advertised set that the setting allows,
  and the Medium Attachment Unit (MAU) type of the negotiated mode.
  Expected with a fixed speed: "Disabled", advertised [none], and the MAU type of the forced
  mode.
      auto-negotiation rows ...... 4 of 4 as expected  (C1, C2, C3, C9: Enabled, the allowed set,
                                   the negotiated MAU)
      fixed-speed rows ........... 5 of 5 as expected  (C8, C4, C5, C6, C7: Disabled, [none],
                                   the forced MAU)
      reverse direction .......... 4 of 4 as expected  (C4-C7, decoded on the DUT)
      back at auto ............... the 4050 decodes the same values as C1
      error lines ................ 0 lines starting with "% " from either unit

  Settings and link status, in the order run. All ports read "connected", except where a
  note says otherwise.
